  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Ohio received $5.7B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Ohio registered 48,170 awards with an average award size of $119K. Ohio federal contract obligations are down 41.9% year over year. Annual totals have varied: Obligations by fiscal year were $8.4B in FY21, $7.8B in FY22, $10.1B in FY23, $9B in FY24, $9.8B in FY25, and $5.7B in FY26. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $3.7B so far in FY2026. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences is the largest industry by obligations at $911M, followed by Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing at $502M and Petroleum Refineries at $498M. General Electric Company is the top vendor at $564M, followed by Marathon Petroleum Company LP at $452M and Southern Ohio Cleanup Company LLC at $322M. All figures are contract obligations; current-year totals are partial and fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies buy the most federal contracts in Ohio?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $3.7B in FY2026 to date. The General Services Administration obligated $462M and the Department of Energy obligated $450M. The Departments of Veterans Affairs and Homeland Security obligated $319M and $154M respectively, with NASA at $151M and other agencies making up smaller shares."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which vendors and industries receive the most federal contract dollars in Ohio?",
        "body": "GENERAL ELECTRIC COMPANY received $564M, MARATHON PETROLEUM COMPANY LP received $452M, and SOUTHERN OHIO CLEANUP COMPANY LLC received $322M in FY2026 to date. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences received $911M, Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing received $502M, and Petroleum Refineries received $498M."
      }
    ]
  },
